(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-cms-exam-2-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 3-day-old neonate has marked bilateral lid oedema and copious purulent discharge. A palpable preauricular node is present. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Topical acyclovir","Oral erythromycin at home","Topical antibiotic drops as an outpatient","Observation with lid hygiene","Hospitalise and give systemic ceftriaxone"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, with specialty consultation — the untreated risk is corneal perforation. A single dose. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"A conjunctival elevation is red at the surface and paler at its base. Which two diagnoses does this favour?","choices":["Trachoma and adult inclusion disease","Uveitis and scleritis","Dacryoadenitis and dacryocystitis","Chlamydial and viral conjunctivitis","Bacterial and allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. That is a PAPILLA. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which demographic does optic neuritis most typically affect?","choices":["Children under 10","Women aged 18 to 45","Men over 60","Men aged 18 to 45","Women over 70"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — about 75% are female. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 22"},{"q":"When are antibiotics indicated in an orbital floor fracture?","choices":["Only in children","Only if the skin is broken","In every fracture","Never","When there is blood in the sinus"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The maxillary sinus. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which follow-up does a corneal abrasion require?","choices":["Review only if vision worsens","No follow-up once analgesia is given","Immediate surgical repair","Periodic re-examination","Annual review"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside a topical broad-spectrum antibacterial. To confirm healing and exclude infection. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with diabetes has ptosis and an eye deviated down and out, but the pupil is normal in size and reactive. What does the pupil finding suggest about the cause?","choices":["A compressive aneurysm","A microvascular ischaemic cause","Myasthenia gravis","Thyroid eye disease","A cavernous sinus lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pupillary fibres run peripherally and are spared by ischaemia but compressed by an aneurysm. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man was struck by a shard of glass. Examination shows a full-thickness corneal laceration with iris prolapsing through it. What must NOT be attempted?","choices":["Keeping the patient nil by mouth","Giving intravenous antibiotics","Checking tetanus status","Repositing the prolapsed iris","Placing a rigid shield"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — manipulation risks further extrusion and infection; shield and refer. In the emergency department. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has non-exudative macular degeneration. What is the single most important advice?","choices":["Reduce salt intake","Wear sunglasses outdoors","Take daily aspirin","Stop smoking","Increase screen brightness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it comes ahead of supplementation. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which demographic does idiopathic intracranial hypertension primarily affect?","choices":["Overweight women of childbearing age","Postmenopausal women","Young children","Men over fifty","Elderly men"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a corneal abrasion. Which symptoms are described?","choices":["Painless loss of the peripheral field","Flashes and floaters","Itching with stringy discharge","Halos with nausea and vomiting","Foreign body sensation, tearing, photophobia"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — one of the commonest ocular injuries. Blurred vision as well. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with giant cell arteritis is started on treatment. What is the named regimen?","choices":["A single dose of oral prednisolone","Observation with weekly review","Aspirin alone","Intravenous methylprednisolone, then a taper","Topical corticosteroid drops"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — typically six to twelve months, with famotidine for ulcer prophylaxis. Three days, then a slow oral taper. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with a tonic pupil is otherwise well. What is the management?","choices":["High-dose corticosteroids","Urgent neuroimaging for an aneurysm","Reassurance, once causes are excluded","Surgical iridoplasty","Immediate carotid imaging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is benign, though the anisocoria may persist. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man describes an episode of 'fleeting blindness' in one eye that lasted about four minutes with complete recovery. Which investigation does every such patient receive?","choices":["Lumbar puncture","Magnetic resonance angiography","Gonioscopy","Colour vision testing","Temporal artery biopsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — with carotid Doppler if a carotid source is suspected and echocardiography if cardiac. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which agent constricts an Adie tonic pupil, and why?","choices":["Acetazolamide, by reducing aqueous production","Atropine, by blocking the sphincter","Timolol, by lowering pressure","Dilute pilocarpine","Phenylephrine, by stimulating the dilator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Through denervation supersensitivity. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman who grew up in a region with poor sanitation has scarring of the tarsal conjunctiva with lashes turned inward and a corneal opacity. What organism is responsible?","choices":["Chlamydia trachomatis","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Adenovirus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Trachoma progresses through follicles, tarsal scarring, entropion with trichiasis, and corneal opacity. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is treated for acute angle-closure in one eye. What is done about the other eye?","choices":["Patching for a week","Long-term topical steroids","Prophylactic iridotomy","Nothing, unless it becomes symptomatic","Immediate cataract surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the fellow eye shares the crowded anatomy. The fellow eye is anatomically at risk. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is chemosis?","choices":["Blood underneath the conjunctiva","A yellowish nodule at the limbus","Inflammation of the lid margin","Swelling of the conjunctiva itself","A ring of vessels around the cornea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a sign rather than a diagnosis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which muscles does the third cranial nerve supply?","choices":["All extraocular muscles","Lateral rectus only","Superior oblique only","Orbicularis oculi","Four of the recti and the levator"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, along with the pupillary sphincter. Medial, superior and inferior rectus, inferior oblique and levator. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which systemic findings accompany the Argyll Robertson pupil?","choices":["Jaw claudication and scalp tenderness","Tabes dorsalis with posterior column loss","Absent ankle reflexes alone","Proptosis and lid lag","Anhidrosis of the face"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — classically tertiary syphilis. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man has a painful, watering red left eye with blurred vision. Fluorescein staining shows a branching epithelial lesion with raised edges and small bulbs at the ends of each branch. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Corneal abrasion","Herpes zoster keratitis","Anterior uveitis","Herpes simplex keratitis","Bacterial corneal ulcer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The true dendrite branches, has elevated edges and terminal end bulbs — pathognomonic. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has herpes zoster ophthalmicus with keratitis. Which treatment is indicated if there is retinitis, choroiditis or optic neuritis?","choices":["Topical antibiotic drops alone","Oral antihistamines","Steroids and intravenous acyclovir","Laser photocoagulation","Observation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman reports that letters look smeared at every distance, and her vision does not clear with a pinhole in one meridian. What is the underlying problem?","choices":["An irregularly curved cornea","An eye that is too long","An eye that is too short","A stiffened crystalline lens","Opacity of the lens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — astigmatism focuses light along two different meridians. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which glands are involved in an internal versus an external hordeolum?","choices":["Both involve the lacrimal sac","Internal is the lacrimal gland; external the meibomian","Both involve the conjunctival goblet cells","Internal is the ciliary body; external the iris","Internal meibomian; external Zeis or Moll"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which structures does ANTERIOR uveitis involve?","choices":["The conjunctiva","The choroid and retina","The iris, or the iris and ciliary body","The sclera","The cornea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — iritis, or iridocyclitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has pre-septal cellulitis. She is systemically well and her parents are reliable. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Oral antibiotics with early review","Observation without antibiotics","Immediate surgical drainage","Admit for intravenous antibiotics","Topical antibiotic drops alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct for mild pre-septal disease in a well child with reliable follow-up. Review at 24 to 48 hours. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 28-year-old worker has a chemical splash to the eye. What is the single most important action?","choices":["Copious irrigation before anything else","Instilling topical antibiotics first","Checking visual acuity first","Patching the eye and referring","Computed tomography of the orbit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The referral-timing slide lists chemical injury as EMERGENT and says IRRIGATE FIRST. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the commonest cause of an isolated fourth nerve palsy, even in adults?","choices":["Multiple sclerosis","Aneurysm","Giant cell arteritis","Congenital","Intracranial tumour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — acquired causes include trauma, even minor. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has bitemporal hemianopia. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":["A lesion at the optic chiasm","A cataract","An optic nerve glioma","A retinal detachment","An occipital infarct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bitemporal (heteronymous) hemianopsia is the chiasmal pattern. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with idiopathic intracranial hypertension asks what is at stake if it is not treated. What should she be told?","choices":["Permanent hearing loss","Permanent loss from optic nerve damage","Development of cataract","Retinal detachment","Progression to a brain tumour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the vision, not the headache, is what drives treatment. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with optic neuritis asks about her vision. What is the expected course?","choices":["Vision recovers fully within 24 hours","The other eye always becomes involved within a month","The condition never recurs","Vision usually recovers substantially over weeks","Vision is permanently lost in most patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, though colour desaturation may persist. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which supplement formulation is named for dry macular degeneration?","choices":["Omega-3 alone","Vitamin A alone","AREDS2 supplementation","Iron and folate","Calcium and vitamin D"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vitamins C and E, zinc, copper, lutein and zeaxanthin. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with optic neuritis is found to have two demyelinating lesions on imaging. What follows?","choices":["Topical pressure-lowering drops","Neurology referral with corticosteroids","Immediate surgery","Observation alone","Temporal artery biopsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — steroids if a demyelinating cause is found. Neuro-ophthalmology is equally appropriate. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has had xanthelasma removed by laser ablation and asks about the outlook. What should he be told?","choices":["The lesions will become malignant if untreated","Recurrence is common despite treatment","Removal is permanent in almost all patients","His vision will deteriorate if they return","They only recur if lipids are normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why the underlying metabolic issue matters more than the local removal. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man's optic nerve shows an increased cup-to-disc ratio with rim thinning and splinter haemorrhages. Which condition do these indicate?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Papilledema","Non-arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy","Central retinal vein occlusion","Chronic open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — with rim pitting and bayoneting on the same list. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 39-year-old landscaper has a yellowish raised nodule on the nasal conjunctiva at the 3 o'clock position of the left eye. It does not reach the cornea. He asks whether treatment will make it go away. What should he be told?","choices":["It controls symptoms but will not resolve it","Surgery is required in all cases","It will become malignant if untreated","It will resolve once sun exposure stops","Lubricating drops will dissolve it over months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sun, dust and wind protection with lubricating drops. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has severe pain in the left eye, decreased vision and nausea. He denies trauma. He wears glasses. On examination the left pupil is moderately dilated and nonreactive, the cornea is 'steamy', and the eye is diffusely red. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Presbyopia","Uveitis","Corneal infection","Conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pain, nausea, a mid-dilated fixed pupil and a hazy cornea together are the classic picture. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a suspected corneal abrasion. How is fluorescein used?","choices":["Injected intravenously and photographed","Taken orally an hour beforehand","Mixed with anaesthetic and swallowed","Applied as an ointment overnight","Instilled and viewed under a blue light"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — it pools in epithelial defects and reveals abrasions, ulcers and foreign bodies. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has a brown discolouration at the centre of the lens and finds distance vision worse than near. Which cataract type is this?","choices":["Cortical","Polar","Posterior subcapsular","Zonular","Nuclear"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the myopic shift is why some patients read without glasses again. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 57-year-old smoker has a droopy right upper lid and a small right pupil that reacts to light. The right side of his face is dry. He has had a cough for three months. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what must be excluded?","choices":["Third nerve palsy, and an aneurysm must be excluded","Myasthenia gravis, and a thymoma must be excluded","Adie tonic pupil, and syphilis must be excluded","Argyll Robertson pupil, and diabetes must be excluded","Horner syndrome, and an apical lung tumour must be excluded"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis; a Pancoast tumour is the concern with this history. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which inheritance feature makes retinoblastoma easy to miss on family history?","choices":["It is always sporadic","It is caused by an infection","It is X-linked","It only occurs in adults","The mutation is often recessive"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — so the family history may be unremarkable. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man was struck in the eye with a squash ball. Vision is hand movements only. Examination shows a peaked, teardrop-shaped pupil and a deep anterior chamber. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Corneal abrasion","Hyphema alone","Orbital floor fracture","Globe rupture","Anterior uveitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A peaked pupil points toward the rupture site; the chamber deepens with a posterior rupture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why can a hyphema be more than it appears?","choices":["It indicates optic nerve injury","It can be a sign of open globe","It always means the retina is detached","It always indicates a skull fracture","It indicates a chemical exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — blood in the chamber warrants looking for a full-thickness defect. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 24"},{"q":"What causes dermatochalasis?","choices":["Trauma","Thyroid disease","Ageing","Chronic infection","Allergy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — excess flaps or folds of skin, bilaterally. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man was grinding metal without eye protection and may have a retained fragment. Which imaging must be AVOIDED?","choices":["Ocular ultrasound without pressure","Magnetic resonance imaging","Plain orbital radiograph","Computed tomography of the orbit","Chest radiograph"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A magnetic field can drag a metallic fragment through the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has a central retinal artery occlusion. Which additional feature would make you suspect giant cell arteritis as the cause?","choices":["Photophobia alone","Recent contact lens use","Jaw claudication and scalp tenderness","A history of migraine","Bilateral itching"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the arteritic cause changes management to immediate high-dose steroids. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 49"},{"q":"A patient presents with acute vision loss. Which four questions does the initial assessment turn on?","choices":["Onset, duration, severity and relieving factors","Age, sex, ethnicity and family history","One or both eyes, sudden or gradual, painful or not","Trauma, infection, inflammation and neoplasm","Visual acuity, pressure, pupil and fundus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those four separate almost the whole differential before any test. Central or peripheral as well. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the management of papilledema itself?","choices":["Topical pressure-lowering drops","Observation without investigation","Treat the underlying disorder","Immediate optic nerve surgery","High-dose corticosteroids in all cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the disc swelling is a sign, not the disease. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has watering of the left eye and a red, exposed inner lid surface. Examination shows the lower lid margin turned outward. She had a facial nerve palsy last year with incomplete recovery. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Age-related entropion","Seventh nerve palsy causing ectropion","Seventh nerve palsy causing entropion","Cicatricial change from prior infection","Dermatochalasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Of the two malpositions, a seventh nerve palsy causes ectropion only. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 34-year-old welder has a superficial corneal foreign body removed. What must be checked before he leaves?","choices":["His refraction","His visual fields","His colour vision","That there is no deeper injury","His intraocular pressure only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a superficial fragment can accompany a penetrating one. No second, penetrating injury. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does a conjunctival melanoma differ in appearance from a conjunctival nevus?","choices":["Both look identical","The melanoma is always amelanotic","The nevus is always painful","The melanoma is raised; the nevus flat with cysts","The melanoma is flat and the nevus raised"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the cysts are the characteristic benign feature. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has a hordeolum that has not improved after two weeks of warm compresses. What is the next step?","choices":["Refer to ophthalmology for incision and drainage","Continue compresses for a further two months","Perform curettage in clinic","Reassure and take no action","Start oral corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — persistence beyond two weeks is the stated threshold. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with chronic blepharitis asks whether treatment will cure it. What should he be told?","choices":["It is chronic and controlled rather than cured","Surgery is definitive","It resolves spontaneously within a month","It resolves once the associated rosacea is treated","A two-week antibiotic course is curative"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Refer to ophthalmology if several weeks of treatment fail. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has noticed over two years that straight lines look wavy and she needs more light to read. Vision is 6/12 bilaterally. Fundoscopy shows yellowish deposits at the macula with pigmentary change but no haemorrhage. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Cataract","Dry age-related macular degeneration","Wet age-related macular degeneration","Open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drusen and pigment change with gradual central loss over years. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has severe purulent conjunctivitis with a palpable preauricular node. Why is the node significant here?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis otherwise lacks a node","It is an incidental finding of no significance","It indicates allergic disease","It confirms a viral cause","It indicates orbital cellulitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe purulent discharge WITH a node points to gonococcal disease. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has severe pain, tearing and photophobia in the right eye after a fingernail scratch. Fluorescein shows a well-defined epithelial defect with no infiltrate. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical anaesthetic drops to take home","Topical corticosteroids","A tight pressure patch for a week","Oral acyclovir","Topical antibiotic ointment and analgesia"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, with follow-up to confirm healing. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 3-year-old girl with unilateral retinoblastoma is referred. Why does the referral need to be urgent rather than routine?","choices":["The eye will become painful within days","It is a life-threatening malignancy","The squint will become permanent","The cataract will mature","The other eye will be affected within a week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — delay risks metastatic spread. Not only a threat to sight. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with dry macular degeneration is given a grid of straight lines to check at home. What is she being asked to detect?","choices":["Raised intraocular pressure","Distortion or a missing area","A change in colour vision","Progressive lens opacity","Peripheral field loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the Amsler grid is a self-monitoring tool for exactly that change. That suggests conversion to the wet form. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with inflammatory dacryoadenitis is started on corticosteroids. Within what period should inflammatory disease respond?","choices":["Six hours","It is not expected to respond to steroids at all","48 hours","Two weeks","One month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — failure to respond in that window prompts reconsideration. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which infection is classically associated with light-near dissociation and small irregular pupils?","choices":["Herpes zoster","Toxoplasmosis","Lyme disease","Syphilis","Tuberculosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — neurosyphilis is the classic association. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has a flat, pigmented iris lesion that has been unchanged on photographs over five years, with no distortion of the pupil. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Immediate enucleation","Topical chemotherapy","Photographic monitoring","Excisional biopsy of the iris","Urgent systemic staging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Documented stability over years supports a benign nevus. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with entropion has developed a corneal epithelial defect from lash contact. What does this change?","choices":["The cornea is now involved","It indicates infection","Surgery is no longer possible","It confirms the diagnosis is ectropion","Lubricants should be stopped"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the slit lamp question in these lid malpositions is always whether the cornea has been affected. Which is what the examination looks for. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has sudden vision loss that is PAINFUL. Which two diagnoses in this block should come to mind first?","choices":["Central retinal artery occlusion and retinal detachment","Amaurosis fugax and papilledema","Acute angle-closure glaucoma and optic neuritis","Non-arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy and branch artery occlusion","Central and branch retinal vein occlusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those are the two painful causes; the rest are painless. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has confirmed acute angle-closure glaucoma. Which anatomical feature predisposed her to it?","choices":["A long axial length","A deep anterior chamber","A large corneal diameter","A thin cornea","A shallow anterior chamber"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — a crowded angle is what allows closure when the pupil dilates. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 29-year-old patient has two days of painful red eyes and blurred vision. The pain is a deep ache with sensitivity to light (photophobia). There is no discharge and no recent trauma. The medical history includes ankylosing spondylitis. Examination shows conjunctival injection primarily around the limbus, a sluggish pupillary response, and cells in the anterior chamber on slit-lamp examination. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Oral acyclovir","Topical corticosteroids","Topical antibiotics","Artificial tears","Topical antihistamines"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with a cycloplegic for comfort and to prevent synechiae. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is found on routine examination to have a raised pigmented choroidal lesion with orange pigment and associated subretinal fluid. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent ophthalmology referral","Refer for cataract surgery","Photograph and review in two years","Reassure, as choroidal pigmentation is common","Start topical corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Orange pigment, thickness and subretinal fluid are the features that separate melanoma from a benign nevus. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 45"}]};
